{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-871078616ea3e55c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Component: ogx-distribution\n2\t\n3\t## Metadata\n4\t\n5\t- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/ogx-distribution.git\n6\t- **Version**: ed8cf8bc319873a583086654087f9f845794d24d\n7\t- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n8\t- **Languages**: Unknown\n9\t- **Deployment Type**: Unknown\n10\t- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n11\t\n12\t## Purpose\n13\t\n14\t**Short**: OGX distribution is a Red Hat-customized build of the OGX (formerly llama-stack) AI agent gateway that exposes multi-provider inference, RAG, and agentic APIs over HTTP on port 8321. [source: distribution/config.yaml:257, distribution/entrypoint.sh:79]\n15\t\n16\t**Detailed**: ogx-distribution packages the OGX framework as a containerized Python service for RHOAI. It acts as a unified AI agent gateway that routes inference requests to configurable remote providers (vLLM, AWS Bedrock, IBM WatsonX, Azure, Vertex AI, OpenAI, Gemini, Anthropic), provides vector I/O through Milvus, PGVector, or Qdrant backends, and offers tool runtime capabilities including web search and file operations. The distribution uses PostgreSQL for both key-value and SQL-backed persistence of agent state, conversations, batches, and metadata. Authentication is handled via OAuth2 token validation with JWKS when configured. The container image is built on an AIPCC base image with Python dependencies installed from the RHEL AI Python Package Index via Hermeto-prefetched packages, and ships pre-fetched Docling and embedding model artifacts. [source: distribution/config.yaml:1-280, Dockerfile.konflux:1-58, distribution/entrypoint.sh:1-79]\n17\t\n18\t## Architectural Analysis\n19\t\n20\tOGX distribution follows a provider-plugin architecture where each API capability (inference, vector I/O, tool runtime, files, batches, responses, messages) is backed by one or more configurable providers. Providers are conditionally activated via environment variable guards (e.g., `${env.VLLM_URL:+vllm-inference}`), meaning the running configuration adapts to the deployment context without code changes. The distribution strips several inline providers (docling, faiss, milvus-inline, sentence-transformers) from the upstream build configuration, shipping only remote-capable providers suitable for production multi-worker deployments. [source: distribution/config.yaml:4-12, build/build.yaml:1-5]\n21\t\n22\tThe entrypoint resolves secret environment variables from `_FILE`-suffixed paths before launching the `ogx run` command, supporting Kubernetes secret volume mounts as an alternative to direct environment variable injection. This pattern avoids exposing secrets via `/proc/1/environ`. OpenTelemetry instrumentation is conditionally enabled when `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` is set, exporting traces and metrics via OTLP. [source: distribution/entrypoint.sh:1-79]\n23\t\n24\tAuthentication is opt-in: when `AUTH_ISSUER` is configured, the server validates OAuth2 bearer tokens against a JWKS endpoint with configurable TLS verification and audience checking. The access policy implements resource-level authorization with owner-based isolation — authenticated users can create resources, and only owners can modify or delete them. System resources (unowned) are readable by all authenticated users. [source: distribution/config.yaml:259-280]\n25\t\n26\tStorage is centralized on PostgreSQL with two backend types: `kv_postgres` for key-value stores (registry metadata, agent state, vector store namespaces) and `sql_postgres` for relational data (inference logs, conversations, batches, file metadata, connectors). This dual-backend pattern allows the framework to use the appropriate access pattern for each data type while sharing a single database instance. [source: distribution/config.yaml:198-235]\n27\t\n28\t## Provenance\n29\t\n30\t### Repo Lineage\n31\t\n32\t| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n33\t|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n34\t| Upstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/ogx-distribution | auto_merge | main | -- | sync_config |\n35\t| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/ogx-distribution | auto_merge | main | `test-upstream-in-showroom.yml` | local_analysis |\n36\t\n37\t### Aliases\n38\t\n39\t| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n40\t|------------|-------------|----|-------|\n41\t\n42\t## Architecture Components\n43\t\n44\t| Component | Type | Purpose |\n45\t|---------|----|-------|\n46\t| Dockerfile.konflux.in:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [ \"/opt/app-root/entrypoint.sh\" ] |\n47\t| Dockerfile.konflux:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [ \"/opt/app-root/entrypoint.sh\" ] |\n48\t\n49\t## APIs Exposed\n50\t\n51\t### Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs)\n52\t\n53\t| Group | Version | Kind | Scope | API Role | Purpose |\n54\t|-----|-------|----|-----|--------|-------|\n55\t\n56\t### Serving Runtime Definitions\n57\t\n58\t| Name | Kind | API Group | Version | Scope | Supported Model Formats | Container Images | Built-in Adapter | Source |\n59\t|----|----|---------|-------|-----|-----------------------|----------------|----------------|------|\n60\t\n61\t### HTTP Endpoints\n62\t\n63\t| Path | Method | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n64\t|----|------|----|--------|---------|----------|----|-----|-------|\n65\t| /v1/inference/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Multi-provider inference routing |\n66\t| /v1/responses/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Agentic responses with persistence |\n67\t| /v1/messages/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Anthropic Messages API passthrough |\n68\t| /v1/batches/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Batch inference processing |\n69\t| /v1/vector-io/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Vector database operations |\n70\t| /v1/tool-runtime/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Web search and MCP tool execution |\n71\t| /v1/files/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | File upload and management |\n72\t| /v1/file-processors/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Document processing (auto-detect) |\n73\t\n74\t### gRPC Services\n75\t\n76\t| Service | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n77\t|-------|----|--------|---------|----------|----|-----|-------|\n78\t\n79\t## Dependencies\n80\t\n81\t### External Dependencies\n82\t\n83\t| Component | Version | Required | Role | Purpose |\n84\t|---------|-------|--------|----|-------|\n85\t\n86\t### Internal Platform Dependencies\n87\t\n88\t| Component | Interaction Type | Role | Purpose |\n89\t|---------|----------------|----|-------|\n90\t| PostgreSQL | SQL/KV client | Required | Key-value and relational storage for agent state, conversations, batches, metadata, inference logs, and vector store metadata |\n91\t| vLLM | HTTP client | Optional | Primary inference backend for LLM and embedding model serving |\n92\t\n93\t## Network Architecture\n94\t\n95\t### Services\n96\t\n97\t| Service Name | Type | Port | Target Port | Protocol | Encryption | Auth | Exposure |\n98\t|------------|----|----|-----------|--------|----------|----|--------|\n99\t\n100\t### Ingress\n101\t\n102\t| Name | Type | Hosts | Port | Protocol | Encryption | TLS Mode | Exposure |\n103\t|----|----|-----|----|--------|----------|--------|--------|\n104\t\n105\t### Egress\n106\t\n107\t| Destination | Port | Protocol | Encryption | Auth | Purpose |\n108\t|-----------|----|--------|----------|----|-------|\n109\t\n110\t## Security\n111\t\n112\t### RBAC - Cluster Roles\n113\t\n114\t| Role Name | API Group | Resources | Verbs |\n115\t|---------|---------|---------|-----|\n116\t\n117\t### RBAC - Role Bindings\n118\t\n119\t| Binding Name | Namespace | Role | Service Account |\n120\t|------------|---------|----|---------------|\n121\t\n122\t### Secrets\n123\t\n124\t| Secret Name | Type | Purpose | Provisioned By | Auto-Rotate |\n125\t|-----------|----|-------|--------------|-----------|\n126\t\n127\t### Authentication & Authorization\n128\t\n129\t| Endpoint | Methods | Auth Mechanism | Enforcement Point | Policy |\n130\t|--------|-------|--------------|-----------------|------|\n131\t| OGX API | All | OAuth2 bearer token (JWKS) | OGX server (built-in) | Owner-based resource isolation: read system resources; create for authenticated; read/update/delete for owners |\n132\t\n133\t### Security Evidence\n134\t\n135\t| Kind | Target | Detail | Signal Type |\n136\t|----|------|------|-----------|\n137\t\n138\t### FIPS Compliance\n139\t\n140\tThis is a Python application built on an AIPCC base image. FIPS compliance depends on the base image's OpenSSL configuration and the Python `cryptography` library's binding to the system OpenSSL.\n141\t\n142\t#### Application-Level Crypto\n143\t\n144\t| Aspect | Value | Source |\n145\t|--------|-------|--------|\n146\t| **Crypto libraries** | cryptography==50.0.0 (OpenSSL binding via cffi) | distribution/requirements-lock-konflux.txt:129 |\n147\t| **TLS configuration** | Per-provider `tls_verify` flags; defaults to `true` for vLLM providers; JWKS auth uses configurable `verify_tls` | distribution/config.yaml:31,265 |\n148\t| **Certificate handling** | System trust store (inherited from UBI base); configurable CA paths for Milvus (`ca_pem_path`, `client_pem_path`) and auth (`tls_cafile`) | distribution/config.yaml:89-91,268 |\n149\t| **Non-FIPS crypto risks** | No explicit FIPS opt-in detected; FIPS mode depends on base image OpenSSL configuration; `--insecure` flag passed to `ogx run` in entrypoint (controls application-level behavior, not TLS) | distribution/entrypoint.sh:79 |\n150\t\n151\t_The Python `cryptography` library binds to system OpenSSL via cffi. On a FIPS-enabled UBI base image, OpenSSL operates in FIPS mode and Python crypto operations use FIPS-validated algorithms. No explicit FIPS opt-in or non-FIPS algorithm usage was detected in the distribution configuration. The `--insecure` flag on `ogx run` is an OGX framework parameter controlling internal validation behavior, not a TLS or FIPS bypass._\n152\t## Data Flows\n153\t\n154\t### Flow 1: Inference Request\n155\t\n156\t| Step | Source | Destination | Port | Protocol | Encryption | Auth |\n157\t|------|--------|-------------|------|----------|------------|------|\n158\t| 1 | Client | OGX Server | 8321/TCP | HTTP | Platform-delegated | OAuth2 bearer token (when configured) |\n159\t| 2 | OGX Server | Inference Provider (vLLM / cloud) | Configurable | HTTP/HTTPS | TLS (configurable per provider) | API token / IAM / bearer token |\n160\t| 3 | OGX Server | PostgreSQL | 5432/TCP | PostgreSQL | Platform-delegated | Username/password |\n161\t\n162\t### Flow 2: RAG Query\n163\t\n164\t| Step | Source | Destination | Port | Protocol | Encryption | Auth |\n165\t|------|--------|-------------|------|----------|------------|------|\n166\t| 1 | Client | OGX Server | 8321/TCP | HTTP | Platform-delegated | OAuth2 bearer token (when configured) |\n167\t| 2 | OGX Server | Vector DB (Milvus / PGVector / Qdrant) | Configurable | HTTP/gRPC/SQL | Configurable (mTLS for Milvus) | Token / API key / password |\n168\t| 3 | OGX Server | Inference Provider | Configurable | HTTP/HTTPS | TLS | API t