t branch nodes are conditional. Let me use plain `E001 --> BA_E005`? Underscores... I'll just write `E001 --> A-E005`? Hmm, mermaid: `A-E005` might parse \"A\" minus... Safer: `BRANCH_A_E005`. Or use square-bracket labels: `BA[\"BRANCH-A-E005\"]`. I'll do:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: provisioning go/no-go\"]\n    E002[\"E002 OGX pgvector provider enablement\"]\n    E003[\"E003 Gen AI Studio BFF vector store wiring\"]\n    E004[\"E004 Benchmark + sizing\"]\n    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E005 Operator-based provisioning\"]\n    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E005 BFF-managed provisioning\"]\n    BC[\"BRANCH-C-E005 Bring-your-own surface\"]\n    E002 --> E003\n    E002 --> E004\n    E001 --> BA\n    E001 --> BB\n    E001 --> BC\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\nTable: Edge | Rule | Rationale. Plus non-edges justification.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n| HLR | Priority | Description | Epic(s) |\n\n## Staff Engineer Override Log\nTable of overrides.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n## Tiered Delivery\nTier 1 / Tier 2.\n\n## Conditional Branches\nDocument the three branch outcomes.\n\n---\n\nNow epic bodies. Required sections: Description, Scope, Acceptance Criteria, HLR Traceability. Title one line at top (# Title).\n\nE001 body:\n\n# Go/no-go decision: default vector store provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5\n\n## Description\nResolves the central unknown ... This investigation produces a decision record that selects the provisioning approach for the default pgvector-backed vector store that Gen AI Studio ships in RHOAI 3.5, and defines the go/no-go gate criteria... The strategy depends on RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) to provide a RHOAI-controlled operator where PostgreSQL lifecycle management can live. Until that dependency's delivery timing for 3.5 is known, the provisioning approach cannot be fixed. Bounded outcomes: (A) operator-based self-provisioning ... (B) gen-ai BFF-managed provisioning ... (C) bring-your-own PostgreSQL.\n\n## Scope\n- Track RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery status/timeline relative to the RHOAI 3.5 release plan\n- Define the go/no-go gate criteria (per sprint planning) ...\n- For each candidate approach, record viability assessment: operator-based (model-registry-operator pattern; requires the modularized operator layer), BFF-managed (mirrors guardrails infrastructure provisioning; lifecycle caveats: cleanup on playground deletion, pod restart handling, credential rotation; portability to the platform-shared database expected in RHOAI 3.6), bring-your-own (users configure the OGXServer spec; aligns with OGX 1.0 breaking changes)\n- Produce decision record: selected approach, rationale, gate criteria and decision date, fallback\n- Confirm scoping model for the default vector store (per-namespace isolation is the stated preference; Auto Rag decoupled per RHAISTRAT-7824)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A decision record exists that selects exactly one provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5 and records the go/no-go gate criteria and the decision date used\n- The record documents the delivery-status assessment of RHAISTRAT-7164 against the 3.5 timeline that the decision was based on\n- The record documents the fallback approach if the selected path fails late (per Staff Engineer input: BFF-managed provisioning or bring-your-own PostgreSQL)\n- The scoping decision (per-namespace vs per-instance) is recorded, with per-namespace as the default unless the assessment surfaces a blocker\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (out-of-the-box default vector store): this investigation determines which provisioning approach delivers it\n- HLR-4 (multi-tenant scoping): confirms the per-namespace scoping model\n\nHmm — HLR Traceability section should list which HLRs this epic covers. OK.\n\nE002 body:\n\n# Enable and validate the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution\n\n## Description\nThe OGX distribution already ships a provider-plugin vector I/O architecture with remote-capable backends; the pgvector backend (remote::pgvector, activated via ENABLE_PGVECTOR) is the selected default vector store backend... This epic enables and validates that provider path for Gen AI Studio playground RAG workloads, establishing the configuration contract (provider activation, connection parameters) that playground provisioning consumes. Per Staff Engineer input, the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable — the work is validation and configuration, not new provider development. Application storage (storage.kv.type / storage.sql.type) remains on its existing SQLite default; vector storage is an independent configuration.\n\n## Scope\n- Verify the remote pgvector vector_io provider activates correctly in the OGX distribution configuration (ENABLE_PGVECTOR guard) with an external PostgreSQL connection\n- Validate vector insert/query round-trips through the /v1/vector-io API against a PostgreSQL instance with pgvector enabled\n- Document the configuration contract: environment variables / connection parameters required to activate the provider, and secret-resolution behavior (_FILE-suffixed paths)\n- Confirm no coupling to OGX application storage backends (SQLite default unchanged); no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration path is in scope\n- Confirm vector data isolation semantics within the provider (vector store namespaces) to support per-namespace scoping\n- Confirm the provider runs decoupled from the OGX process (remote SQL client, not an inline provider)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- With the pgvector provider configuration applied and a reachable PostgreSQL (pgvector-enabled) instance, the OGX server exposes vector I/O through /v1/vector-io and insert/query round-trips succeed\n- The documented configuration contract is sufficient to activate the provider without code changes (environment-variable guarded, following the distribution's existing provider activation pattern)\n- OGX application storage configuration is unchanged from its default when only the vector_io pgvector provider is configured (demonstrated by inspection/configuration test)\n- The provider connects to PostgreSQL as a separate process over the PostgreSQL protocol (no in-process/embedded vector store)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (playground RAG functions with default vector store): provider-level validation of pgvector vector I/O\n- HLR-3 (separate process, not inline): remote pgvector provider decoupled from the OGX instance\n- HLR-4 (multi-tenant scoping): vector store namespace isolation semantics\n\nE003 body:\n\n# Wire the default pgvector vector store into Gen AI Studio playground provisioning\n\n## Description\nThe Gen AI Studio backend (gen-ai BFF) creates OGXServer instances for playgrounds... This epic extends that provisioning so RAG-enabled playgrounds are configured to use the remote pgvector vector_io provider, consuming a PostgreSQL connection secret available in the playground namespace. The wiring is approach-invariant: the secret may be produced by operator-based self-provisioning, BFF-managed provisioning, or a user-provided (bring-your-own) connection — the consumption contract is the same. Users require no manual setup for RAG workflows when a default provisioned instance is available.\n\n## Scope\n- Extend the gen-ai BFF's OGXServer creation path (llamastack_config.go) to configure the pgvector vector_io provider (ENABLE_PGVECTOR plus connection parameters) for RAG-enabled playgrounds\n- Define and consume the PostgreSQL connection-secret contract in the playground namespace (secret name/keys), resolving credentials via the OGX _FILE secret-resolution pattern\n- Preserve the existing application storage configuration (SQLite) on created OGXServer instances — only vector_io configuration changes\n- Preserve the configuration path to external vector databases (remote Milvus/Qdrant/PGVector providers remain selectable), keeping a migration path from the default store to production-grade vector databases\n- Rollback: reverting to the prior OGXServer configuration restores previous behavior; vector data is re-ingested on next use (no data migration required)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A playground created with RAG enabled gets an OGXServer instance configured with the pgvector vector_io provider pointing at the namespace PostgreSQL connection secret, with no manual user configuration steps\n- Playground RAG workflows (document upload, embedding, retrieval-augmented generation) function end-to-end against the default vector store with no loss of current capability (feature-parity checklist against the inline-provider experience passes)\n- The OGXServer application storage setting is unchanged (SQLite default) when the vector store wiring is applied\n- Replacing the default vector store with an external vector database connection is possible through configuration only (no code change), demonstrating the migration path\n- Disabling the default vector store configuration reverts playgrounds to prior behavior without requiring data migration\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (out-of-the-box): zero-manual-step wiring for RAG-enabled playgrounds\n- HLR-2 (no loss of capability): end-to-end playground RAG parity\n- HLR-3 (separate process): provider config targets the remote pgvector backend\n- HLR-6 (migration path): external vector DB config path preserved\n\nE004 body:\n\n# Benchmark the default vector store: inline Milvus baseline versus pgvector\n\n## Description\nCaptures the inline Milvus baseline and verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude on representative playground RAG workloads... dev-experimentation scope per Staff Engineer input: strict latency SLAs are not required; typical playground usage involves small document sets where flat scan (no index) is sufficient. Also validates PostgreSQL resource sizing, using the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits as the starting point.\n\n## Scope\n- Define representative playground RAG workloads (small document sets, flat scan / no index)\n- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline on those workloads (latency and throughput, order-of-magnitude comparison)\n- Measure pgvector-backed vector I/O on the same workloads via the OGX vector-io API\n- Record resource consumption (CPU/memory) of the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance under the workloads\n- Compare against model-registry-operator PostgreSQL resource requests/limits as sizing starting point; recommend adjustments if needed\n- Publish a benchmark report with results and sizing recommendation\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Benchmark report exists covering both the inline Milvus baseline and pgvector measurements on identical representative workloads\n- pgvector measurements are within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline for the measured latency and throughput metrics (report states pass/fail per metric)\n- The report includes a resource-sizing recommendation for the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance derived from measured consumption and the model-registry-operator reference sizing\n- Any measured deviation beyond an order of magnitude is documented with a follow-up recommendation (index selection or sizing change)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (no loss of capability): order-of-magnitude performance comparison against the inline baseline\n- HLR-5 (lightweight): resource consumption measurement and sizing recommendation\n\nBranch epics. BRANCH-A-E005:\n\n# Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n\n## Description\nSelected when the RHAISTRAT-7164 dashboard operator modularization lands in time for RHOAI 3.5: the RHOAI-controlled operator layer self-provisions the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio, following the model-registry-operator's auto-provisioning pattern. No custom image build or pipeline onboarding is required — the Red Hat postgresql-16 image includes pgvector out of the box, and the related-image environment variable is already present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs.\n\n## Scope\n- Implement PostgreSQL/pgvector instance provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled operator for Gen AI Studio (the operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization): Deployment, Service, Secret (credentials), and persistent storage, per playground namespace\n- Use the Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included); wire the related-image environment variable already present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installations\n- Apply resource requests/limits referencing the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point\n- Provision one instance per playground namespace (per-namespace isolation for multi-tenant environments)\n- Produce the PostgreSQL connection secret in the playground namespace conforming to the consumption contract the gen-ai BFF wiring uses\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL (DSC-managed, RHOAI 3.6+) is available, the consumer can switch to the shared connection string\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Creating a RAG-enabled playground in a namespace results in a running PostgreSQL (pgvector-enabled) instance provisioned by the operator, with no manual user steps\n- The provisioned instance exposes a connection secret in the playground namespace matching the documented consumption contract\n- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned PostgreSQL instance and its persistent data\n- Resource requests/limits applied to the provisioned instance match the documented sizing reference, with any deviations recorded\n- The provisioned image reference resolves via the related-image environment variable (disconnected-install compatible), not a hardcoded registry path\n- Vector data in one namespace is not visible from another namespace's instance (per-namespace isolation verified by test)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (out-of-the-box): operator self-provisioning, zero manual steps\n- HLR-3 (separate process): PostgreSQL runs as its own deployment, decoupled from the OGX server\n- HLR-4 (multi-tenant scoping): one instance per namespace with isolated data\n- HLR-5 (lightweight): sizing per model-registry-operator reference\n\nBRANCH-B-E005:\n\n# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the Gen AI Studio backend\n\n## Description\nFallback selected when the operator layer is not available for RHOAI 3.5 and the team keeps the zero-manual-setup experience: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, mirroring how it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure. Creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace. Explicit design attention per Staff Engineer input: the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, and credential rotation need explicit design; and the implementation must be portable to consume a platform-shared connection string when it becomes available in RHOAI 3.6.\n\n## Scope\n- Implement BFF-managed creation of the PostgreSQL (pgvector-enabled) Deployment, Service, PVC, and credential Secret when a RAG-enabled playground is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace\n- Use the Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included) with the related-image reference for disconnected installs\n- Apply resource sizing per the model-registry-operator reference\n- Design lifecycle handling without a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, recovery/handling on BFF and PostgreSQL pod restarts, credential rotation approach\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL is available (RHOAI 3.6+), the BFF consumes the shared connection string instead of self-provisioning\n- Produce the connection secret conforming to the consumption contract used by the playground vector store wiring\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace without an existing suitable PostgreSQL instance results in the BFF creating a running pgvector-enabled instance and connection secret, with no manual user steps\n- Deleting the playground triggers cleanup of the BFF-provisioned PostgreSQL resources (deployment, service, PVC, secret) — cleanup behavior verified by test\n- BFF restart and PostgreSQL pod restart are handled without orphaned or duplicated instances (verified by test)\n- The provisioning path is behind a configuration switch allowing consumption of an externally provided connection string (platform-shared database) instead of self-provisioning\n- Resource requests/limits match the documented sizing reference\n- Per-namespace isolation verified: vector data in one namespace is not visible from another\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (out-of-the-box): BFF self-provisioning, zero manual steps\n- HLR-3 (separate process): separate PostgreSQL deployment\n- HLR-4 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace instance\n- HLR-5 (lightweight): sizing reference\n\nBRANCH-C-E005:\n\n# Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration surface for Gen AI Studio RAG\n\n## Description\nDegraded fallback selected when neither operator-based nor BFF-managed self-provisioning can ship in RHOAI 3.5: customers bring their own PostgreSQL for playground RAG capabilities, manually configuring the OGXServer custom resource spec with their PostgreSQL connection details (explicit connection-string secret). This aligns with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0, though the playground largely abstracts OGX from users. This branch does not satisfy the out-of-the-box acceptance criterion — it is a consciously accepted limitation recorded in the go/no-go decision.\n\n## Scope\n- Expose configuration for a user-provided PostgreSQL connection (connection-string secret) in the playground/OGXServer configuration surface\n- Document the required pgvector extension prerequisite on the user-provided PostgreSQL instance and the supported PostgreSQL versions (Red Hat PostgreSQL 16+ includes pgvector)\n- Keep the playground abstraction: users configure a database connection, not OGX internals\n- Record the accepted limitation (manual setup required) in user-facing release notes\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A user can supply a PostgreSQL connection secret through the configuration surface, and a RAG-enabled playground uses it as its vector store with no code changes\n- Documentation states the pgvector prerequisite and validated PostgreSQL versions, and the steps to configure the connection\n- Release notes state that RAG capabilities require a user-provided PostgreSQL in this release (manual setup), with the planned default provisioned store referenced for the following release\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-6 (migration path): the bring-your-own surface is also the path to production-grade external vector databases\n- HLR-3 (separate process): user-provided PostgreSQL is decoupled from the OGX instance\n- (Partial) HLR-1: explicitly does not deliver the out-of-the-box experience — accepted limitation per the go/no-go decision\n\nSignal rationale files — 7 markdown tables.\n\nNow frontmatter sets:\n\nE001:\n```\npython3 scripts/frontmatter.py set artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md \\\n    epic_id=\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\" title=\"Go/no-go decision: default vector store provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5\" parent_strat=\"RHAISTRAT-8004\" \\\n    component=\"Gen AI Studio\" team=\"Gen AI Studio\" \\\n    type=Investigation priority=P0 dependencies=\"\" \\\n    investigation_signals.question_specificity=1 \\\n    investigation_signals.source_accessibility=0 \\\n    investigation_signals.local_runnability=1 \\\n    investigation_signals.cluster_hardware_dependence=-1 \\\n    investigation_signals.human_judgment_required=-2\n```\nHmm dependencies=\"\" — empty list. Maybe omit dependencies entirely (default []). I'll omit.\n\nE002: type=Implementation priority=P0 component=\"OGX Core\" team=\"OGX\" 