{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-beb2c08b5b120aaa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004: Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio\n11\t\n12\t## Overview\n13\t\n14\tRHOAI must provide a default vector store available out-of-the-box so Gen AI Studio\n15\tplayground RAG workflows keep functioning when inline providers are removed from the\n16\tLlama Stack (OGX) distribution image (RHAISTRAT-7474). Per Staff Engineer Input, the\n17\tselected backend is PostgreSQL with pgvector, consumed through the OGX distribution's\n18\texisting remote `remote::pgvector` vector_io provider. The engineering effort is\n19\tdominated by infrastructure and provisioning of the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance, not\n20\tby vector_io provider integration (the existing remote vector store implementation is\n21\tlargely reusable). No custom image build, Konflux onboarding, or repo onboarding is\n22\tneeded: the Red Hat `postgresql-16` image already includes pgvector, and the related\n23\timage reference is already present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs.\n24\t\n25\tThe central unknown is the **provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5**, gated on the\n26\tdelivery timing of RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress).\n27\tThis is resolved by an Investigation epic with three bounded outcomes, modeled as\n28\tconditional decomposition branches. The OGX provider enablement, the Gen AI Studio BFF\n29\twiring, and the benchmark are invariant across outcomes and form the Tier 1\n30\t(independent) work.\n31\t\n32\tAuto Rag is decoupled per Staff Engineer Input: pgvector is validated only for Gen AI\n33\tStudio Playground RAG workflows; Auto Rag compatibility is owned by RHAISTRAT-7824.\n34\t\n35\t## Epic List\n36\t\n37\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n38\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n39\t| E001 | Go/no-go decision: default vector store provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5 | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n40\t| E002 | Enable and validate the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | OGX | OGX Core | P0 |\n41\t| E003 | Wire the default pgvector vector store into Gen AI Studio playground provisioning | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n42\t| E004 | Benchmark the default vector store: inline Milvus baseline versus pgvector | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | RAG + Vector DB | P1 |\n43\t| BRANCH-A-E005 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n44\t| BRANCH-B-E005 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the Gen AI Studio backend | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n45\t| BRANCH-C-E005 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration surface for Gen AI Studio RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n46\t\n47\t`epic_count = 7` (4 main-plan epics + 3 conditional branch epics; branch epics are\n48\talternative outcomes of the same E005 provisioning slot, not additive work).\n49\t\n50\t## Dependency DAG\n51\t\n52\t```mermaid\n53\tgraph TD\n54\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: provisioning go/no-go\"]\n55\t    E002[\"E002 OGX pgvector vector_io provider enablement\"]\n56\t    E003[\"E003 Gen AI Studio BFF default vector store wiring\"]\n57\t    E004[\"E004 Benchmark: inline Milvus baseline vs pgvector\"]\n58\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E005 Operator-based self-provisioning\"]\n59\t    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E005 BFF-managed provisioning\"]\n60\t    BC[\"BRANCH-C-E005 Bring-your-own PostgreSQL surface\"]\n61\t    E002 --> E003\n62\t    E002 --> E004\n63\t    E001 --> BA\n64\t    E001 --> BB\n65\t    E001 --> BC\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tBranch epics are conditional alternatives gated by the E001 Investigation outcome;\n69\texactly one of BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 is selected. They are excluded from the main-plan\n70\tcritical path. Longest main-plan chain: E002 → E003 (critical path length 2).\n71\t\n72\t## DAG Justification\n73\t\n74\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n75\t|------|------|-----------|\n76\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The BFF wiring builds against the vector_io provider configuration contract (ENABLE_PGVECTOR activation, connection parameters, secret resolution) that the OGX-side epic establishes and validates. Code builds against an API/config surface — serial. |\n77\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 13 (consuming deliverable) | The benchmark measures the enabled pgvector provider path; it cannot produce results before the provider configuration is validated. |\n78\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate, true gate) | The go/no-go outcome determines whether the operator-based provisioning epic exists at all (`remove` if outcome A is not selected). |\n79\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate, true gate) | The BFF-managed fallback only exists if the operator layer is not available for 3.5 and the team keeps the zero-manual-setup experience (`remove` otherwise). |\n80\t| E001 → BRANCH-C-E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate, true gate) | The bring-your-own surface only exists if neither self-provisioning path can ship in 3.5 (`remove` otherwise). |\n81\t| (no edge) E001 → E002 / E003 / E004 | Rule 14 / Step 5.5 Tier 1 | The provider enablement, BFF wiring (consumes a namespace PostgreSQL connection secret regardless of which path produces it), and benchmark are invariant across all three outcomes — they are the independent partial-value tier and must not be serialized behind the decision. |\n82\t| (no edge) E003 ↔ BRANCH-A/B-E005 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | The selected provisioning epic and the BFF wiring coordinate on the connection-secret contract but live in different areas (operator reconciler vs. BFF provisioning path); coordination risk, not a dependency. Main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics. |\n83\t| (no edge) E002 ↔ E001 | Rule 14 | Provider enablement does not depend on the provisioning decision; it is required in every outcome. |\n84\t| (no onboarding epics) | Staff Engineer Correction 2 | No custom image build, Konflux onboarding, or repo onboarding: `postgresql-16` includes pgvector, and the related image reference is already in the rhods-operator CSV. |\n85\t\n86\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n87\t\n88\tThe strategy's High Level Requirements section resides in an attachment not available\n89\tin this workspace (see Health Warnings); HLRs below are synthesized from the strategy's\n90\tAcceptance Criteria, Success Criteria, and Staff Engineer Input.\n91\t\n92\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (source) | Covering epic(s) |\n93\t|-----|----------|----------------------|------------------|\n94\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure (AC-1) | E003, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 — BRANCH-C-E005 explicitly does not satisfy this (accepted degraded fallback) |\n95\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability (AC-2, scoped to Gen AI Studio per Staff Engineer Input) | E002, E003, E004 |\n96\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX/Llama Stack instance — not an inline provider (AC-4) | E002, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005, BRANCH-C-E005 |\n97\t| HLR-4 | P0 | Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments, per-namespace isolation (AC-5) | E002, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 |\n98\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead (AC-6; Staff Engineer: dev-experimentation scope, order-of-magnitude, sizing per model-registry-operator reference) | E004, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 |\n99\t| HLR-6 | P1 | Migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database (open question; alignment with commonly-used vector DB providers) | E003, BRANCH-C-E005 |\n100\t\n101\tAC-3 (\"Auto Rag functions with the default vector store\") is removed from scope per\n102\tStaff Engineer Input — owned by RHAISTRAT-7824 (logged below). Full coverage: every\n103\tHLR maps to at least one main-plan epic; HLR-1's complete satisfaction additionally\n104\trequires exactly one of branch A or B to be selected.\n105\t\n106\t## Staff Engineer Override Log\n107\t\n108\tStaff Engineer Input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach. Each\n109\toverride and its effect on the decomposition:\n110\t\n111\t| # | Strategy position (AI-generated) | Staff Engineer correction | Effect on decomposition |\n112\t|---|----------------------------------|---------------------------|--------------------------|\n113\t| 1 | OGX/LlamaStackDistribution instances require a PostgreSQL database (existing infrastructure) | OGX defaults to SQLite (`storage.kv.type`/`storage.sql.type: sqlite`); the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite; PostgreSQL for vector storage is new work | Provisioning treated as new work (branch epics); ACs assert application storage remains SQLite |\n114\t| 2 | Custom pgvector image build + pipeline onboarding (1–2 sprints estimated) | `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` includes pgvector; model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it; `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` already in the rhods-operator CSV | No image-build, `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics; estimate eliminated |\n115\t| 3 | Auto Rag is an affected component with its own acceptance criteria | Decouple: validate pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG; Auto Rag compatibility is RHAISTRAT-7824 scope | Auto Rag removed from components, ACs, and validation; HLR-2 scoped to Gen AI Studio |\n116\t| 4 | Open questions on latency thresholds, corpus size ranges, index type | Dev-experimentation scope: no strict SLAs; sprint-1 benchmark captures inline Milvus baseline and verifies order of magnitude; flat scan sufficient for typical small document sets | Open questions removed; E004 carries order-of-magnitude ACs; no index-selection Investigation |\n117\t| 5 | Consolidate vector and application storage in one database; SQLite→PostgreSQL upgrade path | Vector storage (`remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) and application storage are independent configurations; application storage stays SQLite | No storage-consolidation or migration epic; E002/E003 ACs assert application storage unchanged |\n118\t| 6 | Platform-level shared database direction | Shared DSC-managed PostgreSQL not available until RHOAI 3.6; for 3.5 Gen AI Studio must self-provision | Self-provisioning is the 3.5 problem; portability to the 3.6 shared connection string is a design requirement in branch epics |\n119\t| 7 | (Technical approach assumed direct implementation) | RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) is a hard dependency for operator-based self-provisioning; go/no-go gate to be defined in sprint planning; fallbacks: BFF-managed provisioning or bring-your-own PostgreSQL | Investigation E001 with three bounded outcomes → conditional branches A/B/C |\n120\t\n121\t## Health Warnings\n122\t\n123\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking).** The full strategy body is stored as a\n124\t   Jira attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in this\n125\t   workspace. The Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table, HLR list with\n126\t   P0/P1/P2 priorities, Dependencies table, and NFRs were unavailable; HLRs were\n127\t   synthesized from the Acceptance Criteria and Staff Engineer Input, and team\n128\t   assignments were inferred (Gen AI Studio team for dashboard/playground work, OGX\n129\t   team for distribution work). Verify against the attachment before sprint planning.\n130\t2. **Priority note on the benchmark epic.** The strategy is Blocker priority and all\n131\t   its acceptance criteria read as must-have, but the benchmark/validation epic (E004)\n132\t   is P1: Staff Engineer Input explicitly relaxed it to dev-experimentation scope\n133\t   (order-of-magnitude, no thresholds), and no other epic depends on it. The\n134\t   lightweight-resource AC (HLR-5) it verifies is carried as sizing ACs on the\n135\t   provisioning epics.\n136\t3. **Branch C degrades AC-1.** The bring-your-own outcome does not satisfy the\n137\t   out-of-the-box acceptance criterion; if the go/no-go lands there, the manual-setup\n138\t   limitation must be a consciously accepted, release-noted decision (per Staff\n139\t   Engineer Input, aligned with OGX 1.0 breaking changes).\n140\t4. **Scoping-model open question handled as a design decision.** The strategy's open\n141\t   question (per-namespace vs per-instance scoping) is resolved by direction —\n142\t   per-namespace is the stated preference and Auto Rag (the per-instance pressure) is\n143\t   decoupled — so it is recorded as an AC/design decision in the provisioning epics\n144\t   rather than an Investigation. Revisit if RHAISTRAT-7824 surfaces per-instance\n145\t   requirements.\n146\t5. **Migration-path open question handled as acceptance criteria.** Bram Sundqvist's\n147\t   migration-path concern maps to the configuration-only path to external vector\n148\t   databases (OGX already supports remote Milvus/Qdrant/PGVector providers) plus the\n149\t   bring-your-own surface — ACs on E003 and BRANCH-C-E005, not a separate epic.\n150\t6. **Shared-surface coordination risk.** The selected provisioning branch epic and the\n151\t   BFF wiring epic co-design the PostgreSQL connection-secret contract in the same\n152\t   repository (odh-dashboard) — coordination risk, not a DAG dependency (Rule 15).\n153\t\n154\t## Component Mappings\n155\t\n156\tNon-obvious mappings to canonical Jira components (from `.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n157\t\n158\t| Architecture / strategy entity | Canonical component | Note |\n159\t|-------------------------------|----------------------|------|\n160\t| ogx-distribution, ogx-k8s-operator (architecture context) | OGX Core | Distribution-side provider configuration work |\n161\t| odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF, dashboard operator, Gen AI Studio playground | Gen AI Studio | All dashboard/playground-side epics |\n162\t| Vector-store performance benchmark | RAG + Vector DB | Benchmark and sizing validation epic |\n163\t\n164\t## Tiered Delivery\n165\t\n166\tRHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) is an external dependency with\n167\tuncertain timing relative to RHOAI 3.5. Partial value is deliverable without it:\n168\t\n169\t- **Tier 1 — independent of RHAISTRAT-7164:** E002 (provider enablement), E003 (BFF\n170\t  wiring against the namespace connection-secret contract), E004 (benchmark and\n171\t  sizing), plus fallback branches B (BFF-managed provisioning) and C (bring-your-own)\n172\t  which deliver the capability without the operator layer.\n173\t- **Tier 2 — gated by RHAISTRAT-7164:** BRANCH-A-E005 (operator-based self-provisioning\n174\t  following the model-registry-operator pattern in the RHOAI-controlled operator).\n175\t\n176\tTier 1 alone can preserve the out-of-the-box experience (via branch B); tier 2 is the\n177\tpreferred long-term-aligned shape when the dependency lands in time.\n178\t\n179\t## Conditional Branches\n180\t\n181\tThe E001 Investigation has three bounded outcomes; exactly one branch is activated:\n182\t\n183\t| Branch | Outcome | Downstream effect | gate_failure_impact |\n184\t|--------|---------|-------------------|---------------------|\n185\t| A | RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for 3.5 | BRANCH-A-E005: operator-based self-provisioning (model-registry-operator pattern) | remove — fall back to branch B or C |\n186\t| B | Operator layer not ready; zero-manual-setup experience kept | BRANCH-B-E005: BFF-managed provisioning (guardrails-infrastructure pattern) with explicit lifecycle design | remove — fall back to branch C |\n187\t| C | Neither self-provisioning path ships in 3.5 | BRANCH-C-E005: bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration surface (degraded, manual setup) | remove — accepted limitation, release-noted |\n188\t\n189\tBranch epics set `branch` and `gated_by` (RHAISTRAT-8004-E001, also in `dependencies`)\n190\tand are attached to the Investigation as plan documents; they are not standalone Jira\n191\tissues and no main-plan epic depends on them.\n192\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa3b99b53c4dda3f","agent_id":"a97702bc5ac309863","session_id":"738b0e56-7748-46a7-9251-8b7683e92d2f","uuid":"e851a3f2-1d85-486a-9ac9-694e33b3d59b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:35:07.500Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-beb2c08b5b120aaa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
